Format: https://www.debian.org/doc/packaging-manuals/copyright-format/1.0/
Upstream-Name: KerBridge
Upstream-Contact: Jarno Elonen <elonen@iki.fi>
Source: https://kerbridge.org/
Comment:
 The tree is GPL-3-or-later with one deliberate exception: kerbridge-core is
 dual-licensed MIT-or-Apache-2.0, so that a third party can link the protocol
 types this project puts on the wire without taking the whole application's
 terms. DEP-5 reserves the short name Expat rather than MIT, and separates
 alternatives with "or", which is why the stanza below reads "Expat or
 Apache-2.0".
 .
 Nothing is said here about Samba. Neither Policy §12.5 nor DEP-5 reaches a
 package's runtime dependencies: both scope this file to the copyright and
 licensing of the package's own contents, `Depends: samba-ad-dc` puts no Samba
 file inside kerbridge-issuerd, and Built-Using triggers only on incorporation.
 The source-offer obligation the README describes attaches to redistributing
 the Docker images, which carry Debian's Samba binaries -- not to a deb that
 names a dependency.
 .
 The statically linked Rust crates are enumerated in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES,
 shipped in each binary package's documentation directory and generated from
 the lock file rather than written here. No Policy rule and no lintian tag
 requires listing them in this file; MIT, BSD and Apache-2.0 do require their
 notices to travel with a binary copy, and a static musl binary is a binary
 copy. That is a licensing obligation rather than a packaging one, which is
 why it is discharged in a generated file that cannot fall out of step with
 the build.

